<Base title="About the data">
  <h1>About this data</h1>

  <p>
    This site compiles publicly available information about infrastructure projects in
    Bengaluru from government budget documents, agency reports, audit reports and
    published news coverage. Every figure is shown with its source and the date it was
    recorded.
  </p>

  <p>
    Figures are drawn from official documents and media reports as published. They are
    not independently audited by us. Budget allocations, sanctioned amounts, funds
    released and actual expenditure are different things, and we label which is which. An
    allocation is not evidence that money was spent, and a delay is not evidence of
    wrongdoing.
  </p>

  <p>
    Project timelines change. Information is accurate as of the "verified on" date shown
    for each project and may be outdated. We update this site quarterly.
  </p>

  <p>
    We do not allege misconduct by any individual, official, department or contractor.
    Infrastructure delays have many causes, including land acquisition, litigation,
    clearances, funding, contractor capacity and design changes.
  </p>

  <p>
    If you are an agency or individual and believe something here is inaccurate, write to
    [email]. We will review it against our sources and publish a correction where
    warranted. Corrections are logged publicly — see the <a href="/corrections">corrections log</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>How we source and rank confidence</h2>
  <p>Every record carries a confidence level, shown wherever the figure appears:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>High</strong> — official document (budget document, audit report, Assembly reply, RTI response).</li>
    <li><strong>Medium</strong> — reported in credible media, or an agency's own report.</li>
    <li><strong>Low</strong> — announcement only, unverified against a document.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Update cadence</h2>
  <p>
    Updated quarterly, aligned to when the underlying data actually moves: the Karnataka
    budget lands in April, agency annual reports follow, and Assembly sessions produce
    question replies in batches. Suggested sweep windows: April, July, October, January.
    Out-of-cycle corrections are always allowed for factual changes — the quarterly cycle
    governs the systematic re-verification sweep, not the ability to fix something. A
    record not touched for two cycles is flagged in the UI as possibly outdated.
  </p>

  <h2>Explicitly out of scope</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Allegations of corruption, or any claim about <em>why</em> a delay happened.</li>
    <li>Contractor performance rankings.</li>
    <li>Anything sourced only from social media or unattributed claims.</li>
  </ul>
</Base>
